SDL 1.2.15: the frame buffer console driver on a board with a fixed-mode
frame buffer, event devices only, and one console

Against the SDL-1.2.15.tar.gz release from libsdl.org
(sha256 d6d316a793e5e348155f0dd93b979798933fb98aa1edebcc108829d6474aad00).
Apply with: patch -p1 < SDL-1.2.15-korvo-fbcon.patch

Written for the ESP32-S31-Korvo-1 running esp32-s31-linux: Linux 7.2.7 with
simplefb (800x480, RGB565), no mousedev driver, and a shell on tty1 that is
the only way into the machine.  Nothing in it is specific to that board.
The changes to SDL are offered under SDL's own licence, the GNU LGPL 2.1 or
later.

src/stdlib/SDL_iconv.c
    The iconv() call is cast so that it compiles with GCC 14 and later.
    This is the change from SDL-1.2 commit 8a8135d9, as Buildroot carries
    it (package/sdl/0004-src-stdlib-SDL_iconv.c-fix-types-mismatch.patch).

src/video/fbcon/SDL_fbvideo.c
  * A frame buffer that has only one mode is recognised with one request,
    and that mode is the only one listed.  Before, some sixty requests were
    made to find that out, and in text mode the kernel redraws the whole
    console after each one that succeeds.
  * No mouse is no longer an error.  SDL_NOMOUSE now means: do not look for
    one.
  * If the video mode cannot be set, the console is put back in text mode
    before the error is returned, so that the program's message can be read.
  * A hardware surface that there is no video memory for no longer keeps the
    pitch of the screen.  It was then given ordinary memory of its height
    times that pitch: 51,200 bytes for a 24x32 picture on a screen 800 wide.
  * On the way out all of the screen is cleared, not only as many lines as
    the program asked for: a smaller picture is centred, and its last lines
    were left on the screen.
  * On the way out SDL's SIGINT and SIGTERM handlers are taken out, as SDL
    means to do itself: with the handlers below in the way it did not.

src/video/fbcon/SDL_fbevents.c
  * A program started on a virtual terminal stays on it, as one that is not
    root always has.  Before, root was moved to a new terminal.
    SDL_FBCON_NEWVT asks for that again; a program started from elsewhere
    (ssh, a serial line) still gets it, as it has no terminal of its own to
    use.
  * While the console is in graphics mode, the signals that end a program
    are caught (SEGV, BUS, ILL, FPE, ABRT, TERM, HUP, INT, QUIT, PIPE, ALRM,
    USR1, USR2; not one that is being ignored).  The console is put back
    with system calls only, on a stack of its own and with other signals
    held off, and then the signal does what it does.  An exit() without
    SDL_Quit() is covered by atexit().  SIGINT and SIGTERM therefore end the
    program at once: no SDL_QUIT event.
  * If the terminal has been hung up, so that the descriptor SDL holds
    answers EIO, it is opened again by its number to be put back.
  * If the terminal settings or the keyboard mode cannot be read after a
    move to a new terminal, the screen is moved back.
  * Ctrl-Alt-Fn only switches terminals if SDL_FBCON_VTSWITCH is set.
  * The three bytes that the kernel sends for a keycode above 127 are
    skipped.  They were read as three keys.
  * Keymaps that the kernel does not have are not asked for key by key:
    2,048 requests at start-up in place of 65,536, and 128 KB of memory
    that is no longer touched.
  * SDL_FBCON_VERBOSE makes the driver say on stderr which terminal, mouse
    and mode it uses, before it leaves text mode.

src/video/fbcon/SDL_fbevdev.c (new), SDL_fbevents_c.h
    Mice on /dev/input/eventN, for kernels without /dev/input/mice: up to
    four devices that report relative X and Y and have a left button.
    SDL_MOUSEDRV=EVDEV asks for these only; they are looked at first in any
    case, unless SDL_MOUSEDRV or SDL_MOUSEDEV names something else.

